TERMINATION

If you use the software in violation of these terms, that use is not licensed
and your licenses terminate automatically. Where the violation is of
Limitation 1, what is not licensed is the use that exceeds the applicable
maximum, the use made through a deployment lacking a required Commercial
License Key, and any other use to which the violation relates.

The "cure period" for a violation is thirty (30) days from the earlier of the
date you receive notice of the violation from the licensor and the date on
which a person responsible for your use of the software becomes aware of it. A
violation of which neither has yet happened has not yet begun its cure period,
and your licenses do not terminate because of it in the meantime. If you cease
the violation within its cure period, your licenses are treated as never having
terminated because of it. If you do not cease the violation within its cure
period, your licenses terminate at the end of that period. If you cease the
violation after its cure period, your licenses are reinstated with effect from
the date you cease it, unless the licensor, acting reasonably and by notice
given within thirty (30) days of your ceasing it, declines to reinstate them on
the ground that you knew the use was not licensed and continued it.

Ceasing a violation does not license the use that occurred before you ceased
it, and does not affect any right of the licensor in respect of that use,
including the right to charge the fees that would have been payable for it
under the licensor's published pricing for the period concerned.

If, after your licenses have been reinstated or preserved under this section,
you commit a further material violation of these terms and do not cease it
within its cure period, your licenses terminate automatically and permanently.

