DEFINITIONS

The "licensor" is Roxane, Inc., the entity offering these terms, and the
"software" is the software the licensor makes available under these terms,
including any portion of it.

"you" refers to the individual or entity agreeing to these terms.

"your company" means: where you are a legal entity, you together with every
organization that has control over, is under the control of, or is under
common control with you; and where you are an individual, any legal entity,
sole proprietorship, or other kind of organization that you work for, together
with every organization that has control over, is under the control of, or is
under common control with that organization. "control" means ownership of
substantially all the assets of an entity, or the power to direct its
management and policies by vote, contract, or otherwise. Control can be direct
or indirect.

"third party" means any person or organization other than you, your company,
the employees and contractors of you and your company who are working on your
behalf, and the licensor.

"derivative work" means any work based on the software or on any part of it,
including any modification, adaptation, extension, translation, and any work
that incorporates any part of the software.

"your licenses" are all the licenses granted to you for the software under
these terms.

"use" means anything you do with the software requiring one of your licenses.

"trademark" means trademarks, service marks, and similar rights.

"PrivOS Hub" means the PrivOS Hub server component of the software.

"workspace identifier" means the unique identifier that a PrivOS Hub server
assigns to the workspace it serves. A deployment's "deployment identifier" is
the workspace identifier of its PrivOS Hub server.

"deployment" means one running instance of a PrivOS Hub server together with
the services attached to it. Each running instance of a PrivOS Hub server is a
separate deployment, whatever identifier it bears; resetting, replacing, or
duplicating a workspace identifier neither creates nor merges deployments.
Instances of parts of the software that run without a PrivOS Hub server form
part of the deployment they serve; where they serve no deployment, all such
instances operated by you and your company together constitute one deployment.
Use of the software is not outside these terms because a deployment has no
workspace identifier.

"Free Community tier" means use of the software, without a Commercial License
Key, that complies with Limitation 1.

"Active Human User" means a natural person who, on or at any time during the
thirty (30) days preceding any date on which the software is used:

  (a) has accessed or interacted with a deployment, including without a user
      account, as a guest or anonymously, and including through an embedded,
      proxied, or white-labeled interface;

  (b) has directed a bot, integration, application, or AI agent that acts on a
      deployment on that person's behalf; or

  (c) is you, is within your company, is an employee or contractor of you or
      your company, or otherwise performs work for or on behalf of you or your
      company, and has submitted content or a request to a deployment, or has
      had content or a request submitted to a deployment on that person's
      behalf, whether directly, through any other system, or by any route that
      delivers it to a deployment.

A natural person directs an agent under (b) if that person gives the agent
instructions or requests through the software or through an interface to a
deployment, and the agent carries them out on a deployment. Paragraph (c)
governs instructions and requests given through any other system.

Where several natural persons use the software through one user account, each
of them is counted separately. A bot, integration, application, or AI agent is
not itself an Active Human User, and a user account through which no natural
person has acted in that period is not counted.

Notwithstanding paragraphs (a) to (c), a person does not become an Active Human
User only because content that person authored was ingested into a deployment,
including where you or your company ingest work that person created in the
ordinary course of their work without that person submitting it. Nor does a
person who is not described in paragraph (c) become an Active Human User only
because a communication that person addressed to you or your company was
relayed to a deployment.

"Commercial License Key" means a license key issued by the licensor for a
specific deployment under a commercial subscription or trial, which sets the
maximum number of Active Human Users permitted under Limitation 1(b) and may
enable additional features. A Commercial License Key states the number of
Active Human Users it permits; a key that states no number permits ten (10). A
key that has expired or been revoked is not valid or in force and permits
none, and the maximum under Limitation 1(a) continues to apply.

"Partner License" means a separate written agreement with the licensor that
expressly permits hosted or managed services, offering the software under your
own brand, commercial redistribution, or distribution of derivative works.
